A2DP test sink
==============

a2dp_test_sink.py makes a Linux PC with BlueZ look like a Bluetooth
headset with whatever capabilities you give it, and reports what an A2DP
source (Rockbox) does with them:

- the configuration the source picks: codec, sample rate, channel mode,
  SBC bitpool range
- per second while streaming: kbit/s, packets/s, frames per packet, the
  SBC bitpool of the frames sent (last, lowest, highest: shows the dynamic
  bitpool at work), the longest gap between packets, lost packets (RTP
  sequence numbers)
- the AVRCP absolute volume the source sets, and on "v <0-127>" changes
  the volume as the headset's own buttons would

It can save each stream (--record) and prints the ffmpeg command that
turns it into a WAV file. SBC, aptX and aptX HD can be listened to that
way; LDAC streams are measured but have no free decoder.

Needs python3 with dbus-python and PyGObject (python3-dbus, python3-gi),
BlueZ 5.x, ffmpeg for listening.


Running it
----------

PipeWire's WirePlumber registers an A2DP sink of its own, and the source
may pick that one instead. run_test_sink.sh stops WirePlumber, runs the
test sink with the arguments given and starts WirePlumber again when it
quits, by "q", Ctrl-C or otherwise. The PC's desktop audio is off
meanwhile.

    ./run_test_sink.sh --agent --discoverable

a2dp_test_sink.py can also be run by itself; it refuses to while
WirePlumber runs, unless given --force.

--agent accepts pairing without asking, --discoverable lets the player
find the PC. On the player, Bluetooth > Devices > [Pair New Device] and
pick the PC by its Bluetooth name. Once paired, connect from the player's
device menu as with a headset; the PC stays paired, so later runs only
need the script.

Type "v 64" and Enter to set the volume from the sink side, "q" to quit.


Examples
--------

SBC only, as most headsets:

    ./run_test_sink.sh

A headset with a low bitpool ceiling, only 48 kHz:

    ./run_test_sink.sh --rates 48 --sbc-bitpool 2-35

Several codecs, to see which one the player's Codec option picks:

    ./run_test_sink.sh --codecs sbc,aptx,aptxhd,ldac

Record and listen:

    ./run_test_sink.sh --codecs aptx --record /tmp/rb
    ffmpeg -f aptx -sample_rate 44100 -i /tmp/rb-<time>.aptx out.wav

./a2dp_test_sink.py --help lists all options.


Notes
-----

The PC's radio is far better than a cheap headset's, so a test sink shows
the protocol and the encoder, not how the player copes with a weak link.

Vendor codec endpoints are registered with Codec 0xFF, the vendor (company
and codec ID) in the Vendor property and in front of the capabilities.
aptX is sent without an RTP header, as A2DP specifies; aptX HD and LDAC
with one.
